She may well be right on the welfare point, but the exact legal requirement depends on your country and the assurance/audit standard you are signed up to.

For trout and other salmonids, **putting conscious fish straight into ice slurry is widely regarded as an unacceptable stunning/killing method**. It can leave fish conscious while they experience cold shock, stress and progressive loss of function. Major fish-welfare guidance—including WOAH guidance and European scientific welfare opinions—generally treats ice slurry as a **post-stun chilling/handling measure**, not a substitute for an effective stun.

The usual welfare expectation is:

1. **Effective stun first** — commonly percussive or electrical;
2. **Confirm the fish is unconscious/insensible**;
3. Then bleed/kill and use ice slurry for cooling and product-quality handling.

So the auditor is not necessarily inventing an extra step because she is new. If the line previously relied on slurry alone, it is quite possible that it no longer meets the applicable welfare code, customer standard, or auditor interpretation.

That said, **“percussive stunning” may not be the only compliant option**. Depending on throughput and local rules, an appropriately designed and validated **electrical stunning system** may be accepted and can be more practical than manually percussing every trout. A welfare auditor should be able to tell you:

- the exact regulation, code, retailer standard, or certification clause she is relying on;
- whether the requirement is law, a certification/customer requirement, or an audit recommendation;
- whether electrical stunning is an acceptable alternative;
- what effectiveness checks and records are expected.

Ask her for that clause in writing before redesigning the harvest process. But I would not assume that “straight to slurry” is defensible simply because it has been done historically—on current fish-welfare thinking, it is the part most likely to be challenged.
